Upskilling for Tomorrow: The Role of AI in African Education
Research date: 24 October 2026

Africa's young people are entering a world of work where artificial intelligence is changing how businesses operate, how professionals solve problems, and what employers expect from workers. A certificate or university degree still matters, but workers increasingly need practical skills, digital confidence, critical thinking, and the ability to learn throughout their careers.

Education systems face a difficult task. They must prepare learners for new opportunities while addressing existing gaps in literacy, numeracy, teacher support, and access to quality education.

The scale of the challenge is significant. A 2025 report by UNESCO, UNICEF, and the African Union found that more than 100 million children and young people across Africa remained out of school.  The World Bank reported in January 2026 that 86% of children in sub-Saharan Africa could not read and understand a simple text by age 10 before the COVID-19 pandemic. 

World Bank Blogs




Artificial intelligence offers new ways to support learning and develop practical skills. AI-powered tutors, adaptive learning platforms, automated feedback, and digital training tools help learners study at their own pace and practise skills beyond the classroom.

But access to AI does not automatically produce competence or employment. The real opportunity lies in combining technology with strong foundational education, skilled teachers, relevant training, and clear pathways into work.
Africa's education and skills gap
Africa's education challenge involves both access and learning outcomes. Millions of learners struggle to complete school, while many who attend school do not acquire the literacy, numeracy, and technical skills needed for further education or employment.

Employers also need workers who understand digital tools, communicate effectively, analyse information, solve problems, and adapt to changing workplace demands.

The African Union's Continental Education Strategy for Africa 2026–2035 recognises the importance of digital literacy, science, technology, engineering, mathematics, and labour-market skills. It also highlights the need for stronger teacher capacity and more relevant vocational and technical training. 

